// B32, and it is the one that says the previous six fixes were the wrong shape.
//
//     function pass(s: String) -> String { return s; }
//     region r { kept = pass(built); }
//
// `pass(...)` does not ALLOCATE — it returns something already built — so `expr_allocates`
// answered false and every rule consulting it went quiet. The value was region storage all the
// same, and `kept` printed `secret-value` and then `0` once the memory was reused.
//
// **The rule tracked allocation when the property it needed was aliasing**: does this value point
// into the region, however it got there. B20/B21 (growth, field, element), B25 (through a mutable
// parameter), B26 (through a dynamic), B27 (a pattern binding), B35 (a field read) were each read
// as one more construct missing from a list, and each fix was correct and insufficient. Six arms in
// two days. B32 is the property they were all approximating.
//
// Closed by a fourth call-graph property — "may this function's return value alias one of its
// parameters" — sharing the rounds the other three already run in, so it costs no extra pass.
//
// The precision matters as much as the catch: a callee returning a LITERAL, or an Int, or a freshly
// built String, does not alias its argument and must still be accepted. Zero false positives across
// 133 corpus programs and 173 real ones.
function pass(s: String) -> String {
    return s;
}
let mutable kept: String = "";
region r {
    let built: String = "secret-" + "value";
    kept = pass(built);
}
print(kept);
